Z||000020||Fierowrecker||11-15-2001||11:44 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by baptistheart:<BR><B>My first car was a 1968 chevy two door hardtop impala with a 327 not to bad, but compared to todays cars it was a monster.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hey Baptistheart!<BR>I had a 68 Chevy Malibu, Yellow with black vinal top...<BR>307 monster cubic inches  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> power slide tranny, 98 MPH top end POS!<BR>Not bad for high school...<BR>Totalled out Christmas eve 9pm by a garbage truck! That was in 1974...<P><BR>------------------<BR>crash... The Fierowrecker<BR>          <IMG SRC="http://fiero.nl/uploads/crash_sig.jpg">          <IMG SRC="http://fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by Fierowrecker (edited 11-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000021||Fiero*101||11-16-2001||12:20 AM||||Well my first car is my 1986 Fiero 2M6<P>I bought it this past July and it was the second fiero i was looking at. The first one was an 84 SE w/ only 40,000 miles on it and he wanted $2,800 and the one i found at a garage sale (dont laugh) was only $750.00 but the 84 had more problems the trany was going out of it. So im kinda glad i didnt buy it. and instead i got this one  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>but the 84 was in the best shape i have ever seen one that old (around here any way) all of them i have seen so far have been beat up or looks like they have been neglected for years.<BR>So thats my first car.<BR>God i love my car  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Fiero*101<BR>Gold 86 SE 2M6<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/mycar101.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000022||baptistheart||11-16-2001||12:32 AM||||mine was green with a white top, and i got into alot of trouble with that car.||||reg||1
Z||000023||Spiff||11-16-2001||02:57 AM||||My first car was while I was still in high school a New 1987 Mustang GT 5.0. No flame please. It stayed stock for about 1 year then I went to work. First 16" rims (back in 88 they were big), NOS and a Turbo. Kept it until I gave it to my brother in 91. Then I bought a 1990 Eagle Talon TSI AWD, but that's another story<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/passsidesmallpic2_2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000024||Red2m4||11-16-2001||04:22 AM||||1988 plymoth horizon<P>totaled it 3 weeks after getting my liscense.||||reg||1
Z||000025||Nashco||11-16-2001||04:52 AM||||Well, not like it was forever ago for me...I'm still pretty young compared to some on the list.  About five years ago my cousin got tired of having to work on her car all the time, a car our grandpa gave to her.  It was a 1969 bright orange VW convertible bug, with a baja kit.  It was stock ride height and had some really cheesy wheels on it with bald tires.  It still had the stock 1500cc single port engine with a single 31mm carburetor.  I got it right after I got my first "real" job, the summer before sophomore year.<P>I bought it from my cousin for a good price, and paid really low monthly payments...55 bucks a month, but it seemed to take forever <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  It was more of a "low payments are easy, but force you to make cash" kind of deal, to keep me busy and out of trouble.  Well, it worked, because I got hooked.  I'd always liked cars, but didn't know much about VWs.  I started learning everything I could possibly grab onto, which is a lot with VWs.  They have about ten times as much information out there on them as the Fiero, even though they're much simpler.  If you thought the fiero has had a lot of engine swaps done....oh boy, are you in for a shock.  Just about any motor ever imagined has been used in a VW, along with tons of different transmissions.<P>So I started building my VW up, having a major engine failure right before my first summer.  I rebuilt a 1600cc dual port motor, and eventually had a weber progressive two barrel (32mm/36mm) carburetor, stinger exhaust, mallory dual point distributor, and crane hi output coil.  Oh, by the way, I got wheels and tires for Christmas, right before my 16th birthday.  They made the car look a thousand times better!  So after some motor work and big tires, I wanted to finally go offroad!  Well, I went with a buddy in his Jeep, and I got stuck EVERYWHERE.  The next weekend I raised my rear suspension by turning the rear torsion bars and raised it a couple inches.  About a year later I rebuilt the entire frond end with ball joints, tie rods, disc brakes, new bushings, seals, etc. and installed an adjustable front torsion bar setup, making my car about two inches taller in the front as well.<P>After that point, I started getting ready for my move to school.  I have parked the VW back home, and I'm preparing to do an engine swap with it.  I plan on putting a 4.3 Chevy V6 and a Porsche 901 4 speed transaxle.  I've got almost all the parts I need, just a matter of installation when I'm home for the holidays.<P>As much as I hate to admit it, that car is my best love, even more so than the Fiero.  I miss it a ton, and I hate to leave it back home while I'm gone just to sit...especially when I know if I had it to work on it would already have the V6 in it!  I hope to have the engine swap accomplished within the next two years (being realistic).  I want to have a tow bar for both my VW and my Fiero that is easily removed, so that I can always have both of them, using a tow behind dolly.  I couldn't stand to part with one or the other, so I may end up leaving the Fiero back home when my VW is finished.  My dad and brother both like the Fiero a lot, so I know it will be in good hands.  Here's my baby, and thanks for listening to my story guys, it's nice to reminisce <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P> <IMG SRC="http://home1.gte.net/jbnash/brycenbug.jpg"> <P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000026||Return of Fiero||11-16-2001||06:08 AM||||i find it funny how few of us were born and bred with the Fiero. Very surprised with the 23-27 crowd, not many owned the Fiero first. Kind of an odd revolation, dont think i can look at the Fiero the same again. And to think, my first word was Fiero, my first HotWheels was a Fiero, and my first car was a Fiero. It all makes sence, now i know why my wife gets upset when i wear my one peice feeted PJ with the Fiero logo to bed...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>sorry, im just tired, later!<BR>Myke||||reg||12
Z||000027||Oreif||11-16-2001||08:35 AM||||1969 Pontiac Catalina. Commonly known as a "lead sled". Lots of room, I think the trunk was a 10 body trunk.<P>------------------<BR>            <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">          <BR><FONT SIZE="1"><B><I>Happiness isn't around the corner...<BR>Happiness IS the corner.</I></B></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000028||Haagster||11-16-2001||08:48 AM||||1978 Renault LeCar...  OK, stop laughing.  It was a car.  Well...  Sorta.  Think that's why chicks hated me in school?!?  Or maybe it was the holes that were the floorboards...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>-&gt; Traded for a 1980 "Little Red "S"Chovette"  Drove it into the ground.  Left front wheel (still attached to the rotor/suspension etc) fell off over a speed-bump (at the dealer lot) when I drove it to trade-in.<P>-&gt; 1982 Ford Exp (barracks b#tch car at Fort Polk)<BR>-&gt; 1987 Dodge Shadow (lemon law applied)<BR>-&gt; 1990 Ford Ranger (sold for Korea assignment)<BR>-&gt; 1983 Cutlass Ceira (needed a car in Panama)<BR>-&gt; 1983 Kawasaki 750 LTD (broke the car...)<BR>-&gt; 1987 Pontiac Grand AM (wife needed to learn to drive)<BR>-&gt; 1988 Cutlass Ciera (wife didn't want to share the car after she got her license)<BR>-&gt; 1988 Fiero Coupe (wrapped the Olds around a tree at 75MPH)<BR>-&gt; 1987 Fiero GT (traded the coupe, still running.  As a matter of fact, the ONLY car I ever traded-in that was still drivable  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">)<P>------------------<BR>        <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Signature_8.jpg">   <BR>87GT Auto||||reg||6
Z||000029||Formula88||11-16-2001||08:58 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Oreif:<BR><B>1969 Pontiac Catalina. Commonly known as a "lead sled". Lots of room, I think the trunk was a 10 body trunk.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Oooh!  Cool!  My introduction to Pontiacs came from a friend of mine in High School who had a 71 Catalina, ex-patrol car.  It had the 400 instead of a 455, but it sure would move to be a tank.  <P>That Cat didn't happen to have a 428 did it? I'd love to find one of those!||||reg||1
Z||000030||Raydar||11-16-2001||10:12 AM||||First was a 65 Plymouth Valiant 4-door with  170 cubic inches of one-barrel slant-six thunder.     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"> <P>First car that wasn't a hand-me-down was a 68 Fairlane 500 hardtop with a 302. <BR>Dark green. Chrome reverse wheels. <BR>Installed a (too big) Holley carb and Edelbrock Torker intake, and Hooker headers. Dual glasspacks. <BR>Broke two or three C-4 trannies and blew out one rearend. (Young and stupid. What can I say...)<P>Got my first three tickets with that car. <BR>50 in a 35 (BFD)<BR>Laying drags. (Got tagged in the HS parking lot during a burnout contest. <BR>Beat a Formula 400, BTW. Almost worth it.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  This is also when I blew the rearend.)<BR>Excessively loud mufflers.<P>GF at the time called it the "Doomsday Machine".<BR> <BR>Traded it for a 73 Barracuda after the frame cracked. (Not my fault. Power steering bracket required for header clearance caused too much stress.)<P>------------------<BR>Raydar<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>From the Department of Redundancy Department.</FONT><BR><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 11-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000031||kslish||11-16-2001||10:15 AM||||The first car that I actually went out and bought with my hard earned (yea right) money was my red '86 Fiero 2m4 which I still have and it now has 3 other Fieros to share the parking pad with.  <P>However, the first car I actually had was an '88 Eagle Premier that was a hand-me-down from my parents.  A good handling sedan (also was powered by the same PRV V6 that was in the DeLorean) but was a mishmash of unreliable parts from Renault and AMC.  While I had the car, the tranny went (a german ZF unit), the ignition module went (turned out to be made by General Motors), and the fuel pump died which turned out to be made by Bosche.  Truely a "world car" experiment gone wrong.  The engine felt like it had a lot more miles left in it though when I got rid of it (the transmission started acting up again).<P>Also, the first "new" (i.e. not used) car I bought was my black Oldsmobile Intrigue which I bought in September.<P>Well, you never exactly specified which "first" you wanted.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P><P>------------------<BR>Ken S.<BR>'87GT and '86SC<BR>See them at <A HREF="http://homepage.mac.com/kjslish/Fiero.html" TARGET=_blank>http://homepage.mac.com/kjslish/Fiero.html</A><BR>Western PA Fiero Club - <A HREF="http://www.westpennfiero.org" TARGET=_blank>www.westpennfiero.org</A>||||reg||1
Z||000032||Fformula88||11-16-2001||10:18 AM||||Its not a really good story...<P>My first car was a rusted out old 1980 Ford Fairmont wagon.  Huge holes in the fenders, rust spots all over it.  I spent a whole summer doing body work on it.  Lots of Bondo etc, and my uncle painted the thing.  Looked great!  I drove it about three months and then the whole thing started falling apart mechanically.  It developed a bad stalling problem, the exhaust went on it, the defroster fan motor went bad, and the front brakes went.  I took it off the road and got rid of it soon after.  The body work wasn't a total waste of time because now I can do great body work!  I can make body repairs look showroom new now, which has helped me on a couple occasions including painting the wing on my Fiero!<P>------------------<BR>John  <BR>AOLIM: Fformula88<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/formulasig.JPG">         <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">   <P>1988 Fiero Formula<BR>1985 Fiero SE 2M6||||reg||1
Z||000033||bocefas||11-16-2001||10:27 AM||||1977 Z-28, firethorn red(burgandy), 350, 4 bbl, super T-10 4 spd., 3.91 posi rear.  My dad bought the car brand new in '76, gave it to me in '87 on my 16th b-day(with new quarters and a fresh paint job minus the UGLY decals), drove the car until it had 180,XXX miles on it, orig. clutch, alterator, starter, etc, but I had to have the frame repaired twice due to rust through, and after the third time I sold it to a kid for $1500 and bought myself a '89 IROC...the kid had it for about a month or so and fishtailed it into the front of a pickup truck.  I'm not ashamed to say that I cried that day.  Anyway, after the IROC went in the divorce (BI^&H!!!!), I got an '88 2.5 fiero, and then an '86 gt, and an '87 gt, and here I am today!||||reg||1
Z||000034||Jaygee79||11-16-2001||10:31 AM||||My first car was the Fiero that I have now.  I saw an ad on TV for a used car lot that was giving away a free car to the person who needed it most.  So my mother wrote an essay explaining why she thought that I deserved the car.  She won and she had the choice of 2 cars, a Ford Fairmont and a Pontiac Fiero.  She chose well  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000035||Bazooka||11-16-2001||10:42 AM||||******************************<P>      <IMG SRC="http://www.members.aol.com:/m34chev/charge.jpg">      <P><BR>My first was a 1966 Dodge Charger with a 383-4 barrel + 4 speed trans. It had fold down rear seats that were best utilized at the Passion Pit (Drive-Inn Theatre) when reverse parking toward the picture screen. It has plenty of power available. I fixed up the body's minor dents and imperfections (cast aluminum front grill and frt bumper) then painted it a midnight blue. I still kick myself for selling it years later, but we should accept change with a smile. Then I got a 70 AMX with a 390 automatic (2 seater). This was a hint of the Fieros to follow as it felt like it did wheelies every time you stepped on the gas. That was another car I should have held on to. My present rehabilitation is a V8 Fiero GT and a stock Fiero GT. The Fieros are functionally contrasting and well appreciated in the right settings.||||reg||1
Z||000036||SKIDMARK||11-16-2001||10:44 AM||||I bought a 67 BMW 1600 for $300 of my hard earned paper route money. Then I bought a new clutch for $120, new generator (6-volt) for $220, new brakes for $120, and new tires for $200. After 2 months of working on it, I finally got it on the road. 3 months later, someone backed into the front end and pushed the radiator into the fan. Then came winter and the block froze and cracked. Was a great car! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"><BR>My first REAL car was a 68 firebird, competition orange with O/S brushed aluminum deep dish ET's and M-40 racing slicks in the back, 400 4bbl 3spd. Not the fastest thing on the road but fun to drive anyway.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000037||Oreif||11-16-2001||11:06 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Formula88:<BR><B> Oooh!  Cool!  My introduction to Pontiacs came from a friend of mine in High School who had a 71 Catalina, ex-patrol car.  It had the 400 instead of a 455, but it sure would move to be a tank.  <P>That Cat didn't happen to have a 428 did it? I'd love to find one of those!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Nope, it had a 400 with a 2-bbl carb. A friend ahd a Grand Prix SJ with the 428, that thing screammed!||||reg||1
Z||000038||Formula88||11-16-2001||11:24 AM||||The guy who currently has my '69 Firebird also has a mint condition 69 Grand Prix Model SJ with a 428HO and a 4-speed!  From the factory!  That and 4-wheel manual drum brakes.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif">  Man, would I LOVE to get my hands on that car!! <P>He's got lots of GTO's too.  He restores them and sells them.  He bought the Firebird thinking he'd try one out, but lost interest cuz he wasn't really into the car.  I can only hope he finally decided to fix up the car, and perhaps sell it.  I hate the thought of it rotting in his back yard.||||reg||6
Z||000039||Nashco||11-16-2001||12:23 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Oreif:<BR><B> Nope, it had a 400 with a 2-bbl carb. A friend ahd a Grand Prix SJ with the 428, that thing screammed!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Ha, no way!  My best buddy (at the time) had that same exact car for his first car.  We called it "The Land Barge" for obvious reasons.  Did you ever park next to the biggest rig you could find, just to dwarf them? <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  He loved parking next to a Suburban just so he could laugh...the Catalina is longer than a Suburban!!!  I think he measured it at just over 19 feet...but still had fair turning radius for how big it was.  Power it had, I always tried convincing him to make it fast with some headers, big carb, intake, etc. but he didn't want to because it would be a waste on such an un-customizable car.  He had a point..I couldn't see it lowered, big wheels, raked out, pretty paint, etc..just wouldn't look right, nothing seemed to fit it except being large and stock <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000040||jeffreyeffrey||11-16-2001||12:32 PM||||'86 fiero 2m4. Dealer actually tried to talk me out of it because of their poor reputation. but I was 15, always wanted a fiero, and there was no way I was going home without that car. Ended up putting some $$ in it but I never regreted it. I've had 4 cars since then and still have an '86GT and always will.||||reg||1
Z||000041||Formula88||11-16-2001||01:27 PM||||Speaking of Catalinas, I remember one I saw back in the 80's.  It was a 73 Catalina two door.  It had huge tires all the way around (probably about 245/60-15) on Crager SS chrome rims.  Dual exhaust with probably glass packs.  And the personaized liscense plate read "YACHTROD".  That was perfect!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
