Z||000058||2birds||11-19-2001||12:36 AM||||My parents were in the Iowa Street Rod Association when I was a teenager back in the early 80's, so I was fortunate enough to have fun in my early driving years.<P>I learned how to drive in a '33 Terraplane 8 coupe, with a 289 Ford and C-4 in it.  Only car we had with an automatic.  Since most rod runs were big keg parties back then, I spent a lot of time as a designated driver with only a learner's permit.<P>My first driver when I was 16 and got my license was a '52 Ford F-1 pickup, metallic gold with brown naugahyde interior.  Powered (quite well) by a Ford 390/4-barrel and C-6 automatic.  I surprised more than a few Chevelles, Mustangs,  and Camaros on the streets of Cedar Rapids, Iowa before I joined the Navy.  The truck was sold at Carlisle two years ago, supposedly to be imported to Holland by a collector of American Muscle cars, so Cliff, be on the lookout.||||reg||1
Z||000059||thomas_l||11-19-2001||08:20 AM||||My first one was a 67 Mustang that I bought from a guy at school for $300. When I drove it home, there was gum plugging a hole in the oil filter, one operating front brake, and manual shift the automatic in first & second but no drive. I spent all my spare time & money when I was 15 getting it road-ready. It had a later 302 with headers and those Thrush mufflers you can see through. Big fun was driving thru town and switching the ignition off to make it backfire. With a 6-cyl rear end it would hold its own street racing but topped out at 110. No I don't want that car back  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> it was a POS when I owned it and an even bigger POS when I unload.. er sold it.||||reg||10
